  const communityPassage = `A — Oakfield Community Centre: membership
Annual membership costs £24 for adults and £12 for full-time students. Please bring evidence of student status when registering. Membership gives access to the reading room, free use of the public computers and reduced prices for selected classes. It does not include entry to the swimming pool, which is managed separately. You can register at reception on weekdays between 9 a.m. and 5 p.m. Online registration is currently unavailable. New members receive a card immediately; please bring it whenever you visit. Lost cards can be replaced for £3. Membership fees cannot be refunded after the first fourteen days.

B — Saturday bicycle checks
Volunteer mechanics offer basic bicycle checks in the centre's rear courtyard on the first Saturday of each month, from 10 a.m. until 1 p.m. There is no charge for a check, although any replacement parts must be paid for. The team can adjust brakes, repair punctures and check lights. They cannot service electric motors or repair damaged bicycle frames. Children under sixteen must come with an adult. No booking is required, but visitors are seen in order of arrival and the queue may close early if demand is high. Please clean heavily muddy bicycles before bringing them in. The service moves to the covered loading area in wet weather.

C — Evening pottery course
Our six-week introductory pottery course begins on Tuesday 8 September. Classes run from 6.30 to 8.30 p.m. in Room 4. The standard fee is £90, or £75 for centre members, and includes clay, tools and the firing of two small pieces. Extra firing is charged separately. Wear old clothes and closed shoes; aprons are available to borrow. There are twelve places, and advance booking is essential. Payment is required when booking. A full refund is available if you cancel at least seven days before the first class. Later cancellations cannot be refunded, but you may transfer your place to another person after informing the tutor. Completed pieces can normally be collected two weeks after the final class.

D — Neighbourhood walking group
The walking group meets outside the centre every Thursday at 10 a.m. Walks last about ninety minutes and finish at the same place. The routes are mainly on paved paths, with occasional gentle slopes. Please contact the organiser in advance if you need information about step-free access on a particular route. Participation is free and centre membership is not required. Bring water and suitable footwear. The group continues in light rain, but a walk may be cancelled during thunderstorms or an official severe-weather warning. Cancellations are posted on the centre's website by 8.30 a.m. on the day. Dogs are welcome if kept on a lead. There is no need to register for an individual walk.

E — Room bookings and building access
Local groups may hire meeting rooms for educational or social activities. Room 1 seats up to twenty people and has a projector. Room 2 seats twelve and is the only meeting room with direct access to a small kitchen. Both rooms are on the ground floor and have step-free entrances. Booking requests should be emailed at least five working days before the event. A request is not confirmed until the centre sends written acceptance. Groups must leave rooms tidy and remove their own rubbish. The building closes at 9 p.m., so evening bookings must end by 8.45 p.m. to allow time to pack away. Furniture may be rearranged but must be returned to its original position.`;
