  const workPassage = `A — Starting work at Northgate Distribution
On your first morning, report to reception at 8.30 a.m. and ask for your team supervisor. Bring photographic identification and your bank details. You will receive an access badge after completing the registration form. The badge opens the staff entrance and records your arrival and departure. Never lend it to another person. If you forget it, reception will issue a temporary badge for that day. A lost badge must be reported immediately so that it can be disabled. The company supplies any protective footwear required for warehouse duties; office staff do not need to buy this footwear.

B — Training and daily duties
All employees attend a general safety induction during their first week. Warehouse staff must also complete practical training before operating lifting equipment, even if they have used similar equipment elsewhere. Only an authorised trainer can sign off this training. Until then, new staff may observe an experienced operator but must not operate the equipment themselves. Your supervisor will explain the daily task list at the morning briefing. If a task seems unsafe or the instructions are unclear, stop and ask for guidance. Meeting a delivery deadline never takes priority over safe working procedures.

C — Breaks and staff facilities
Employees working more than six hours receive an unpaid thirty-minute meal break. Supervisors allocate break times so that essential services remain covered. Please discuss changes with your supervisor rather than swapping times informally with a colleague. Food must be eaten in the staff dining area, not at packing stations. A refrigerator and microwave are available. Label food containers with your name and remove any unused food by Friday afternoon. The refrigerator is cleaned on Friday evening, and anything remaining is discarded. Lockers are available for personal belongings, but the company cannot provide storage for bicycles inside the building. Bicycle racks are located beside the staff entrance.

D — Absence and schedule changes
If illness prevents you from coming to work, telephone your supervisor before your shift begins. A text message alone is not sufficient. Explain when you expect to return, if known, and agree when to provide an update. Medical information is handled confidentially. Planned leave requests should be submitted through the staff portal at least two weeks in advance. Submitting a request does not mean it has been approved; wait for confirmation before making non-refundable travel arrangements. Requests are considered in the order received, taking account of staffing needs. During busy periods, some dates may be unavailable.

E — Reporting problems and improving work
Report accidents and near misses promptly, even if nobody is injured. A near miss is an event that could have caused harm but did not. The purpose of reporting is to identify risks and prevent future incidents. An online form is available on the staff portal, and a supervisor can help if you cannot access it. For urgent hazards, tell a supervisor immediately rather than waiting to complete the form. Suggestions for improving work can be placed in the box beside the dining area or sent through the portal. The operations manager reviews suggestions monthly and posts a summary of actions on the staff noticeboard. Employees may submit suggestions without giving their names.

F — Visitors and deliveries
All visitors must sign in at reception and wear a visitor badge. They must remain with an employee while in operational areas. Drivers delivering goods should use Gate 3, where the receiving team will provide unloading instructions. The main staff entrance is not a delivery point. Employees expecting a visitor should inform reception in advance and arrange to meet the visitor there. Personal parcels may be delivered to reception, but staff must collect them by the end of the next working day because storage space is limited.`;
